description:
Implement CrlChecker: an offline RevocationChecker that validates a DER-encoded CRL and checks certificate serial numbers against the revoked list.

BLOCKED BY: PKIX-0kd (Error type), PKIX-b9z (RevocationChecker trait must return pkix_revocation::Result).

Design:
  pub struct CrlChecker {
    crl_der: &[u8],  // or owned Vec<u8> behind alloc feature
    // need current time for CRL validity check — design decision needed:
    // option A: pass now_unix at construction time
    // option B: add a now_unix field, set it separately
    // option C: ignore CRL validity for v0.1 (document limitation)
    // Recommend option A for simplicity
    now_unix: u64,
  }

  impl RevocationChecker for CrlChecker {
    fn check_revocation(&self, cert, issuer) -> Result<(), Error> {
      1. Parse self.crl_der as a CertificateList (x509-cert CRL type or raw DER)
      2. Verify CRL signature: extract signatureAlgorithm and signature, verify against issuer.tbs_certificate.subject_public_key_info (need SignatureVerifier or inline crypto — TBD, may need to pull in a backend or accept a SignatureVerifier parameter)
      3. Check thisUpdate <= now_unix <= nextUpdate → Err(CrlExpired) if outside
      4. Walk revokedCertificates list; if cert.serial_number matches → Err(Revoked { serial, reason_code })
      5. Return Ok(())
    }
  }

Note on step 2 (CRL signature): CrlChecker may need to accept a SignatureVerifier for CRL signature verification, OR it can be limited to RSA/ECDSA inline. Design TBD — file a follow-up if this becomes complex.

Test vectors: generate with OpenSSL:
  openssl ca -gencrl -out crl.pem && openssl crl -in crl.pem -outform DER -out crl.der
  openssl ca -revoke cert.pem && openssl ca -gencrl -out crl-with-revocation.pem
  Commit DER fixtures.
